\section*{Question 4}

Let \(R\) be the region in the first quadrant enclosed by the graphs of \(y=2 x\) and \(y=x^{2}\), as shown in the figure above.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find the area of \(R\).
\item[(b)] The region \(R\) is the base of a solid. For this solid, at each \(x\) the cross section perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis has area \(A(x)=\sin \left(\frac{\pi}{2} x\right)\). Find the volume of the solid.
\item[(c)] Another solid has the same base \(R\). For this solid, the cross sections perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis are squares. Write, but do not evaluate, an integral expression for the volume of the solid.
\item[(a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text { Area } & =\int_{0}^{2}\left(2 x-x^{2}\right) d x \\
& =x^{2}-\left.\frac{1}{3} x^{3}\right|_{x=0} ^{x=2} \\
& =\frac{4}{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[(b)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text { Volume } & =\int_{0}^{2} \sin \left(\frac{\pi}{2} x\right) d x \\
& =-\left.\frac{2}{\pi} \cos \left(\frac{\pi}{2} x\right)\right|_{x=0} ^{x=2} \\
& =\frac{4}{\pi}
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[(c)] Volume \(=\int_{0}^{4}\left(\sqrt{y}-\frac{y}{2}\right)^{2} d y\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { integrand } \\ 1: \text { antiderivative } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { integrand } \\ 1: \text { antiderivative } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[] \(3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}2: \text { integrand } \\ 1: \text { limits }\end{array}\right.\)
\end{itemize}
